{"key":"apchem-mcq-031","kind":"mcq","model":"google/gemini-3.8-flash","pass":"solve","ok":true,"object":{"chosen_label":"B","final_answer":"A metallic solid, such as a transition metal","working":"Metallic solids consist of metal cations surrounded by a sea of delocalized valence electrons. The presence of freely mobile electrons explains why they conduct electricity efficiently in both solid and liquid states. The non-directional nature of metallic bonding allows planes of atoms to slide over each other under stress without fracturing, making them malleable and ductile. Ionic solids do not conduct electricity in the solid state and are brittle. Covalent network solids are hard, brittle, poor conductors (with few exceptions), and have very high melting points. Molecular solids have low melting points and do not conduct electricity.","defect":""},"usage":{"inputTokens":465,"inputTokenDetails":{"noCacheTokens":465,"cacheReadTokens":0},"outputTokens":354,"outputTokenDetails":{"textTokens":156,"reasoningTokens":198},"totalTokens":819,"raw":{"thoughtsTokenCount":198,"promptTokenCount":465,"candidatesTokenCount":156,"totalTokenCount":819,"trafficType":"ON_DEMAND","promptTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":465}],"candidatesTokensDetails":[{"modality":"TEXT","tokenCount":156}]},"reasoningTokens":198,"cachedInputTokens":0},"attempts":1,"mode":"object","ms":3596,"keyed_label":"B"}
